Older UK men are likely to respond most to threats they see as personally relevant and concrete. Heart disease (D) and bowel cancer with a graphic image (E) fit that best: bowel cancer screening is actively promoted to this age group, and heart disease is a common worry at this stage of life. Diabetes (B) and generic cancer (A) are also relevant, but less so. The "13 types of cancer" claim (C) is abstract and may be less vivid. The generic obesity label with a photo of overweight people from behind (F) gives no specific threat and may provoke defensiveness, so it should have the least effect.

These are judgement-based estimates, not measured effects.

{"points": {"A": 14, "B": 15, "C": 12, "D": 24, "E": 25, "F": 10}, "most_ignored": "F", "why": "The generic obesity label with a photo of unnamed overweight people gives no concrete, personally relevant health threat and may trigger defensiveness, so it is least likely to reduce this audience's desire to eat the bar."}

Under-35 UK women tend to respond most to cancer framing and to vivid, specific imagery. Heart disease and bowel cancer feel more distant at this age, and the photo of overweight people from behind is stigmatising and gives no clear consequence, so it may provoke reactance rather than reduced craving. The "13 types of cancer" claim is broad and alarming, and the cancer surgery photo is vivid without being as gruesome as the cancerous bowel image, which may trigger avoidance in some viewers. I've weighted F and E highest, followed by D, then B, A, and C.

For the "most_ignored" field, I've taken it to mean the label with the least effect on desire.

{"points": {"A": 12, "B": 14, "C": 10, "D": 18, "E": 22, "F": 24}, "most_ignored": "C", "why": "The stigmatising image of overweight people with no specific health consequence is likely to be dismissed or to provoke reactance, while the broad, alarming cancer claim paired with vivid surgery imagery is most likely to cut this audience's desire to eat the bar."}

Women aged 35-54 in the UK are likely to respond most to the cancer warnings. Bowel cancer is a salient concern in the UK, given national screening campaigns and high-profile advocacy, and the graphic image makes it concrete. The "13 types of cancer" claim is specific and surprising, which tends to read as credible and memorable. The generic "cancer" label is alarming but vaguer, so it scores lower. Heart disease and diabetes are serious but feel less immediately threatening to this group than cancer, and the bleeding eye is a strong image that may still provoke disgust-driven avoidance. The label with no health consequence beyond obesity is weakest, since it gives no vivid outcome and may trigger reactance.

These are judgement-based estimates, not measured effects. Graphic images can also cause some people to look away or feel defensive, so the ranking among the middle options is less certain than the top and bottom.

